1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,030
In this series of lectures about the history of weather forecasting,

2
00:00:07,030 --> 00:00:14,600
I'll start by examining its early history - that'll be the subject of today's talk.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:20,020
OK, so we'll start by going back thousands of years

4
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:24,184
Most ancient cultures had weather gods,

5
00:00:24,184 --> 00:00:27,940
and weather catastrophes, such as floods

6
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:32,570
played an important role in many creation myths.

7
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:37,441
Generally, weather was attributed to the whims of the gods,

8
00:00:37,441 --> 00:00:42,800
as the wide range of weather gods in various cultures shows.

9
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:47,040
For instance, there's the Egyptian sun god Ra

10
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:51,950
and Thor, the Norse god of thunder and lightning.

11
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:57,320
Many ancient civilisations developed rites such as dances

12
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:02,280
in order to make the weather gods look kindly on them.

13
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:06,225
But the weather was of daily importance:

14
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:14,115
observing the skies and drawing the correct conclusions from these observations was really important,

15
00:01:14,115 --> 00:01:18,060
in fact their survival depended on it.

16
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:24,730
It isn't known when people first started to observe the skies,

17
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:27,080
but at around 650 BC

18
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:32,613
the Babylonians produced the first short-range weather forecasts,

19
00:01:32,613 --> 00:01:38,070
based on their observations of clouds and other phenomena.

20
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:42,140
The Chinese also recognised weather patterns

21
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:47,236
and by 300 BC, astronomers had developed a calendar

22
00:01:47,236 --> 00:01:51,540
which divided the year into 24 festivals

23
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:55,790
each associated with a different weather phenomenon.

24
00:01:55,790 --> 00:02:03,430
The ancient Greeks were the first to develop a more scientific approach to explaining the weather.

25
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:07,842
The work of the philosopher and scientist Aristotle,

26
00:02:07,842 --> 00:02:12,040
in the fourth century BC, is especially noteworthy

27
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:17,770
as his ideas held sway for nearly 2,000 years.

28
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:22,460
In 340 BC, he wrote a book in

29
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:29,800
which he attempted to account for the formation of rain, clouds, wind and storms.

30
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:34,197
He also described celestial phenomena such as haloes -

31
00:02:34,197 --> 00:02:38,480
that is, bright circles of light around the sun

32
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:42,990
the moon and bright stars - and comets.

33
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:48,000
Many of his observations were surprisingly accurate.

34
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,960
For example, he believed that heat could cause water to evaporate.

35
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:00,075
But he also jumped to quite a few wrong conclusions,

36
00:03:00,717 --> 00:03:05,130
such as that winds are breathed out by the Earth

37
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:10,500
Errors like this were rectified from the Renaissance onwards.

38
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:13,640
For nearly 2,000 years

39
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:19,740
Aristotle's work was accepted as the chief authority on weather theory.

40
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:23,700
Alongside this, though, in the Middle Ages

41
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:28,968
weather observations were passed on in the form of proverbs,

42
00:03:28,968 --> 00:03:32,660
such as 'Red sky at night, shepherd's delight

43
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:37,110
red sky in the morning, shepherd's warning'.

44
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:42,383
Many of these are based on very good observations

45
00:03:42,383 --> 00:03:47,070
and are accurate as contemporary meteorologists have discovered.

46
00:03:47,070 --> 00:03:55,213
For centuries, any attempt to forecast the weather could only be based on personal observations,

47
00:03:55,213 --> 00:04:02,270
but in the fifteenth century scientists began to see the need for instruments.

48
00:04:02,270 --> 00:04:07,378
Until then, the only ones available were weather vanes -

49
00:04:07,378 --> 00:04:13,620
to determine the wind direction and early versions of rain gauges.

50
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:17,880
One of the first, invented in the fifteenth century

51
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,380
was a hygrometer, which measured humidity.

52
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:29,830
This was one of many inventions that contributed to the development of weather forecasting.

53
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:34,660
In 1592, the Italian scientist

54
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:40,030
and inventor Galileo developed the world's first thermometer.

55
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:45,060
His student Torricelli later invented the barometer

56
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:49,120
which allowed people to measure atmospheric pressure.

57
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:57,890
In 1648, the French philosopher Pascal proved that pressure decreases with altitude.

58
00:04:57,890 --> 00:05:04,019
This discovery was verified by English astronomer Halley in 1686;

59
00:05:04,019 --> 00:05:10,760
and Halley was also the first person to map trade winds.

60
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:19,671
This increasing ability to measure factors related to weather helped scientists to understand the atmosphere

61
00:05:19,671 --> 00:05:26,800
and its processes better, and they started collecting weather observation data systematically.

62
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:29,719
In the eighteenth century, the scientist

63
00:05:29,719 --> 00:05:36,045
and politician Benjamin Franklin carried out work on electricity and lightning in particular,

64
00:05:36,045 --> 00:05:40,560
but he was also very interested in weather

65
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:43,830
and studied it throughout most of his life.

66
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:51,420
It was Franklin who discovered that storms generally travel from west to east.

67
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:55,012
In addition to new meteorological instruments,

68
00:05:55,012 --> 00:06:00,400
other developments contributed to our understanding of the atmosphere.

69
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:05,220
People in different locations began to keep records

70
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:06,847
and in the mid-nineteenth century,

71
00:06:06,847 --> 00:06:13,790
the invention of the telegraph made it possible for these records to be collated.

72
00:06:13,790 --> 00:06:20,940
This led, by the end of the nineteenth century, to the first weather services.

73
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:26,680
It was not until the early twentieth century that mathematics

74
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:31,067
and physics became part of meteorology,

75
00:06:31,067 --> 00:06:36,130
and we'll continue from that point next week.
